why did we create the switch in the first place?
we created it because things get so messy when we try to connect a group of computers via cables
its nearly impossible to keep the whole network neatly organized and easily tracked when we connect
a group of computers with each others
--> thats when switches come in!

switches help organize connecting a gourp of computers by provviding a central unit where all computers are connected to 
instead of having to connect computers with each other
---

how does the switch work?

back before we created the switch, we had something called: Hub traffic

say that we have 4 computers connected with other via the Hub traffic: c1, c2, c3, c4

hub traffic is stupid, when you try to send a message from c1 to c2
the message will go from c1 to hub traffic
you expect the hub traffic to send the message to c2
but thtas not the case!
hub traffic sends the message received from c1 to all the computers that are connected to it
it will send it to c2, c3, c4

--> this is a bad thing!

when you send a message to all computers that are connected to hub traffic, this means that all the computer can see the data! 

hub traffic doesnt care about the privacey and securety of the data!

---------
hence switch was made!

when we have 4 computers connected to the same switch
and you want to send a message from c1 to c2
the message will only be sent to c2, and c2 will be able to see it


how does the switch work?

switch is just a device with multiple ports!
it does something like this:

on port 1, thats where the c1 exists
on port 2, thats where the c2 exists
on port 3, thats where the c3 exists
on port 4, thats where the c4 exists

the switch doesnt know that c1 is c1
it recognize c1 by its mac address

